    "evidence_text": "When a sentence is given and encoded into a vector by the encoder, the decoders are trained to predict the next sentence and the previous sentence.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\n0.9, a $\\beta _2$ of 0.999 and a L2 weight decay rate of $0.01$.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.\n\nFor Chinese NLP tasks, we pre-train the model using Chinese corpus.\n\nFor the first 3 Chinese tasks, we follow the settings as in ERNIE .\n\nNSP task to provide more document-level information in the pre-training.\n\nMotivated by the in-symmetric property of NSP, we propose to differentiate between different sentence orders by including PSP.\n\nDespite the simplicity, extensive experiments demonstrate that the model obtains a better ability in pair-wise semantic reasoning.\n\nOur work suggests that the document-level objective is effective, at least for the BERTbase model.\n\nIn the future, we will investigate the way to take advantages of both large-scale training and our method.",
